1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,880
Listen.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,020
Practice.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:09,880
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,470
Business English phrases for email.

6
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:18,310
Yeah, the ones that pop up again and again.

7
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:19,000
Exactly.

8
00:00:19,570 --> 00:00:27,460
It definitely highlights the core stuff for clarity, professionalism, making those emails efficient.

9
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:28,490
Okay, let's get into it.

10
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:30,310
Sharpen those email skills.

11
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:31,060
Absolutely.

12
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:31,700
It's funny.

13
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:37,100
These phrases seem simple, but they're like the oil in the machine of business communication.

14
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:37,740
Oil in the machine.

15
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:38,210
I like that.

16
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:38,610
Yeah.

17
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:39,890
It's not just being polite.

18
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:42,730
It's about, you know, building trust, making things run smoother.

19
00:00:43,070 --> 00:00:45,340
Your word choice really shapes perception.

20
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:45,900
Totally.

21
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:47,460
And that repetition we mentioned.

22
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:48,180
Yeah.

23
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:49,360
These are the building blocks, right?

24
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:50,220
Foundational stuff.

25
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:50,700
Definitely.

26
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:51,060
Yeah.

27
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:51,540
Okay.

28
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:52,270
First block then.

29
00:00:52,890 --> 00:00:53,470
Gratitude.

30
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:57,390
The material kicks off with, thank you for your prompt response.

31
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:01,150
We all say thanks, but why specifically call-out prompt?

32
00:01:01,170 --> 00:01:02,570
Is it really that big a deal?

33
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:03,650
Oh, I think so.

34
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:09,790
Beyond just manners, acknowledging speed, it subtly reinforces that behavior.

35
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:12,380
Ah, like positive reinforcement for quick replies?

36
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:12,900
Exactly.

37
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:17,700
They feel seen, valued, makes them more likely to be quick next time, maybe.

38
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:22,590
Plus, in a crazy inbox world, it shows you value their time, sets a good rhythm.

39
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:23,260
Yeah.

40
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:23,440
Okay.

41
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:23,960
That makes sense.

42
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,160
A little psychological nudge.

43
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:26,000
All right.

44
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,600
Moving on to scheduling.

45
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,950
I am writing to confirm our meeting schedule.

46
00:01:30,630 --> 00:01:31,290
Very direct.

47
00:01:31,430 --> 00:01:32,530
And that's the power, right?

48
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:33,170
Clarity.

49
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:35,590
Business communication hates ambiguity.

50
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:36,680
It wastes time.

51
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:38,840
So starting like that just cuts to the chase.

52
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:40,600
Tells them the email's purpose instantly.

53
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:43,300
They can process it, check their calendar, flag issues.

54
00:01:43,610 --> 00:01:44,450
Respects their time.

55
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:45,790
Ensures everyone's aligned.

56
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:47,090
No last minute panic.

57
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:47,930
Okay.

58
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:54,950
And sticking with that proactive vibe, we also have, let's schedule a follow-up call, discuss progress, and are you available for a brief meeting next week?

59
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:58,110
Yeah, these show you're thinking ahead, keeping the ball rolling.

60
00:01:58,160 --> 00:01:59,360
So let's schedule a follow-up.

61
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:01,460
Shows you're already planning the next step.

62
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:01,820
Right.

63
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:05,060
It signals structure, organization, keeps momentum.

64
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:08,730
And are you available for a brief meeting?

65
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:12,920
Concise, respectful, adding brief is key.

66
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:13,500
Why brief?

67
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:15,220
It signals you're mindful of their time.

68
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:19,340
Makes them, you know, more likely to say yes if they know it won't eat their whole day.

69
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:20,530
Good point.

70
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:22,950
Always be considerate.

71
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:24,230
Now, attachments.

72
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:27,050
Please find attached the latest project report.

73
00:02:27,610 --> 00:02:28,970
We must have typed that a million times.

74
00:02:29,370 --> 00:02:30,370
Why does it work so well?

75
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:31,570
Well, it's standard, isn't it?

76
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:32,380
Predictable.

77
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:33,860
Everyone knows what it means.

78
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:35,520
Like a universal email code?

79
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:36,520
Sort of.

80
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,360
It clearly says document attached.

81
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:39,160
Here's what it is.

82
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:41,110
Less thinking for the recipient.

83
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:43,970
They know where to look, what it is, less chance they miss it.

84
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:44,710
Makes sense.

85
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:52,060
The material also has, I'm reaching out to update you on our project status, and I will update you once more information becomes available.

86
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:53,430
Keeping people in the loop.

87
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:57,810
And transparency is just crucial, especially in projects.

88
00:02:57,830 --> 00:03:01,600
Reaching out to update shows you're proactive, even if it's just a small update.

89
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:02,810
Builds trust.

90
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:05,460
And the, I'll update you when I know more one.

91
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:07,100
Manages expectations.

92
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:08,460
It says, I haven't forgotten.

93
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,760
I'll tell you when I have something concrete.

94
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:13,110
Stops them chasing you unnecessarily.

95
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:14,290
Okay.

96
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:16,610
Now, needing clarification.

97
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:18,270
Happens all the time.

98
00:03:18,790 --> 00:03:21,780
Could you please clarify the deadline for this deliverable?

99
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:25,200
What's the magic in phrasing that kind of question?

100
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:26,380
It's that balance, isn't it?

101
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:28,220
Polite, but direct.

102
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:31,560
Could you please softens it, keeps it respectful.

103
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:34,770
But it still clearly asks for the specific info.

104
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:35,350
Exactly.

105
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:37,970
Way better than being demanding or like super vague.

106
00:03:37,990 --> 00:03:40,020
Gets you a better, faster response usually.

107
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:41,540
And what about when you're really stuck?

108
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:43,930
Please advise on the best course of action.

109
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:45,530
When do you pull that one out?

110
00:03:45,550 --> 00:03:46,670
Strategically, I think.

111
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:52,660
When you genuinely value their expertise or they see the bigger picture, it acknowledges their experience.

112
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:54,870
But you shouldn't just like dump the problem on them.

113
00:03:55,070 --> 00:03:55,850
Probably not, no.

114
00:03:56,270 --> 00:03:57,650
Best to show you've thought about it first.

115
00:03:58,230 --> 00:03:59,070
Give some context.

116
00:03:59,110 --> 00:04:01,520
You don't want to seem totally lost or like you haven't tried.

117
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:01,920
Got it.

118
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:02,980
Do your homework first.

119
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:07,750
Okay, shifting gears to relationship building.

120
00:04:07,770 --> 00:04:10,370
We appreciate your continued support and partnership.

121
00:04:10,390 --> 00:04:11,210
That feels important.

122
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:11,910
It really is.

123
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:13,620
Long-term success.

124
00:04:13,810 --> 00:04:15,540
often relies on strong relationships.

125
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:18,680
Saying that reinforces how much you value that connection.

126
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:20,120
Fosters loyalty, maybe?

127
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:20,780
Definitely.

128
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:26,070
Goodwill, loyalty, encourages more collaboration, shows you don't take them for granted.

129
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:28,050
Simple phrase, big impact.

130
00:04:28,530 --> 00:04:29,350
Yeah, absolutely.

131
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:32,500
And getting feedback, also vital.

132
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,460
Your feedback on this matter would be greatly valued.

133
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:37,320
What makes that phrasing work?

134
00:04:37,470 --> 00:04:42,100
The greatly valued part, it's explicit, tells them you genuinely want their input, that it matters.

135
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:44,790
Makes them feel like it's worth their time to respond thoughtfully.

136
00:04:44,970 --> 00:04:45,490
Precisely.

137
00:04:45,530 --> 00:04:49,480
It's encouraging, respectful, you're more likely to get good insights.

138
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:53,360
Then there's the more direct, please provide your feedback on the attached proposal.

139
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:54,600
A bit different.

140
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:56,570
Yeah, more of a direct call to action.

141
00:04:56,590 --> 00:05:02,260
You've got a specific thing you need eyes on, still polite with please, but very clear about the needed action.

142
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:03,400
Efficient.

143
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:03,660
Okay.

144
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:06,260
Now the not-so-fun stuff.

145
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:07,350
Delivering bad news.

146
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:12,050
We regret to inform you of a delay in our timeline.

147
00:05:12,030 --> 00:05:13,470
How do you handle that professionally?

148
00:05:13,630 --> 00:05:17,780
Clarity and a bit of formality help.

149
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:21,080
Regret to inform is professional direct.

150
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:25,520
Regret acknowledges the negative impact without getting overly emotional.

151
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:26,670
And you can't just leave it there.

152
00:05:27,030 --> 00:05:27,910
Oh, definitely not.

153
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:33,350
You absolutely have to follow up immediately with why there's a delay, what you're doing about it, and what the new timeline looks like.

154
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:36,190
Transparency is key, even with bad news.

155
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:36,650
No sense.

156
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:38,160
OK, confirming receipt.

157
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:40,000
Please confirm receipt of this email.

158
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:42,380
When is this little request most necessary?

159
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:43,820
Critical info, definitely.

160
00:05:44,150 --> 00:05:48,530
Or if there are tight deadlines or important instructions attachments that need action.

161
00:05:48,750 --> 00:05:50,320
So it's like an extra layer of security.

162
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:51,400
Yeah, peace of mind.

163
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:54,250
You know, it didn't just vanish into the ether or get buried.

164
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:56,690
And then the action-oriented ones.

165
00:05:56,710 --> 00:05:59,130
Please confirm if the attached terms are acceptable to you.

166
00:05:59,290 --> 00:06:03,520
And please review and sign the attached contract at your earliest convenience.

167
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:05,250
These are about pushing things forward.

168
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:05,790
Totally.

169
00:06:06,050 --> 00:06:07,810
Direct requests for specific actions.

170
00:06:08,590 --> 00:06:11,770
Confirm if terms are acceptable needs a clear yes or no.

171
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:12,900
And the contract one.

172
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:15,440
It specifies both review and sign.

173
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:20,060
And that, at your earliest convenience, adds a polite push for timeliness.

174
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:20,600
Right.

175
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:21,380
That gentle nudge.

176
00:06:22,410 --> 00:06:24,210
Now, making communication easy.

177
00:06:24,670 --> 00:06:27,770
I am available to discuss this at your earliest convenience.

178
00:06:27,790 --> 00:06:28,670
Offering flexibility.

179
00:06:28,950 --> 00:06:29,810
Good strategy.

180
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:31,200
Yeah, it's considerate.

181
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,730
Shows you'll work around their schedule that you prioritize the conversation.

182
00:06:35,610 --> 00:06:38,680
Less demanding than suggesting a specific time right off the bat.

183
00:06:38,890 --> 00:06:39,480
Okay.

184
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:40,980
On the flip side, good news.

185
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:43,640
We are pleased to announce the launch of our new product.

186
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:46,330
How do you share positive updates professionally?

187
00:06:46,570 --> 00:06:47,710
Well, that phrase is pretty good.

188
00:06:47,870 --> 00:06:49,470
Clear, concise, professional.

189
00:06:49,750 --> 00:06:52,300
Pleased to announce sets that positive tone right away.

190
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:55,140
Great for informing stakeholders, getting people excited.

191
00:06:55,220 --> 00:07:19,760
keeping conversations alive follow-ups I would like to follow up on our previous discussion simple direct what makes a follow-up email good conciseness is key remind them briefly of the context then state the purpose clearly that phrase does it well polite professional prompts recall and gentle reminders like this is a gentle reminder regarding our pending invoice how do you chase money politely

192
00:07:19,790 --> 00:07:22,140
Gentle reminder is the magic phrase there, isn't it?

193
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:23,320
Softens it right down.

194
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:24,760
Avoid sounding accusatory.

195
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:27,320
Keeps the relationship intact while still prompting action.

196
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:29,990
Factual, polite, always best.

197
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:30,250
Right.

198
00:07:30,590 --> 00:07:30,850
Okay.

199
00:07:30,870 --> 00:07:31,650
Acknowledging help.

200
00:07:32,270 --> 00:07:34,910
Your assistance in this project has been invaluable.

201
00:07:35,190 --> 00:07:35,350
Yeah.

202
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:36,480
That feels really strong.

203
00:07:36,650 --> 00:07:36,960
It is.

204
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:39,100
And it has a huge positive impact.

205
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:41,000
Shows deep appreciation for their effort.

206
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:41,940
Boosts morale.

207
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:43,120
Strengthens the team.

208
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:44,320
Makes people want to help again.

209
00:07:44,370 --> 00:07:46,410
Feeling valued is powerful.

210
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:47,180
Absolutely.

211
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:48,350
Now, urgency.

212
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:50,230
Sometimes you just need things fast.

213
00:07:50,330 --> 00:07:55,920
We've got, I would appreciate your urgent attention to this matter, or could you send me the details by the end of the day even?

214
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:59,370
Please prioritize this task as it is critical.

215
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:02,760
And could you expedite the documentation process, please?

216
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:05,760
How do you rush someone without sounding rude?

217
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:09,430
It's about clarity and explaining the why, if possible.

218
00:08:09,830 --> 00:08:14,740
Phrases like urgent attention or critical to our success signal importance clearly.

219
00:08:14,980 --> 00:08:17,290
And specific requests like by end of day.

220
00:08:17,610 --> 00:08:19,810
The host set clear expectations.

221
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:24,620
The key is maintaining that polite tone throughout, even when you're stressing the need for speed.

222
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:25,640
Appreciate.

223
00:08:25,810 --> 00:08:26,210
Could you?

224
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:26,670
Please.

225
00:08:26,810 --> 00:08:27,450
They all help.

226
00:08:27,430 --> 00:08:27,790
Got it.

227
00:08:28,310 --> 00:08:30,410
What about stating company goals or standards?

228
00:08:30,770 --> 00:08:36,160
We are committed to delivering top quality results, or we need to revise our strategy to better meet our goals.

229
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:37,520
Why put those in emails?

230
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:42,470
Sets expectations, communicates values, direction, committed to quality, reinforces standards.

231
00:08:42,770 --> 00:08:48,010
Revised strategy shows you're proactive, adaptable, it aligns everyone, fosters transparency.

232
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:49,740
And closing emails well.

233
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:52,940
Thank you for your understanding and cooperation.

234
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:53,540
Common ending.

235
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:54,800
Why is it effective?

236
00:08:54,930 --> 00:09:00,320
It's polite, professional, especially after you've asked for something or maybe caused a slight inconvenience.

237
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:02,690
Acknowledges their willingness to work with you.

238
00:09:03,470 --> 00:09:04,710
Leaves a good final impression.

239
00:09:05,370 --> 00:09:07,030
Fosters that goodwill we talked about.

240
00:09:07,330 --> 00:09:07,790
Makes sense.

241
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:10,620
What about needing specific expertise?

242
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,960
It would be beneficial to have your expertise on this matter.

243
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:15,520
Respectful way to ask.

244
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:15,990
Very.

245
00:09:16,550 --> 00:09:18,520
It highlights the value their knowledge brings.

246
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:20,240
It's an invitation, not a demand.

247
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:22,840
Makes them feel valued, like their opinion matters.

248
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:23,880
And scheduling again.

249
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:25,050
Offering choices.

250
00:09:25,650 --> 00:09:28,350
Let us know if the proposed dates are suitable for you.

251
00:09:28,370 --> 00:09:29,390
Collaborative approach.

252
00:09:29,410 --> 00:09:29,970
Exactly.

253
00:09:29,990 --> 00:09:37,440
It empowers them, makes it feel less like a dictate, shows you're considering their availability, willing to find a time that works for everyone.

254
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:38,540
Okay, apologies.

255
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:39,710
We all mess up sometimes.

256
00:09:40,690 --> 00:09:43,230
I apologize for any inconvenience this may have caused.

257
00:09:43,690 --> 00:09:44,970
Standard, but important.

258
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:47,270
crucial for trust and relationships.

259
00:09:47,890 --> 00:09:53,120
A clear apology like that acknowledges the impact, shows you're aware, helps mitigate negative feelings.

260
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:55,490
Taking responsibility is key.

261
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:56,920
And needing the green light.

262
00:09:57,700 --> 00:10:00,130
We need your approval to proceed with the next steps.

263
00:10:00,550 --> 00:10:01,150
Very direct.

264
00:10:01,570 --> 00:10:02,730
Anything else needed with that?

265
00:10:02,930 --> 00:10:03,410
Clarity.

266
00:10:03,590 --> 00:10:05,560
Again, state what you need approval for.

267
00:10:05,620 --> 00:10:07,880
Maybe briefly outline those next steps.

268
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:10,560
Provide context so they have all the info they need to decide.

269
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:10,900
Right.

270
