Hoping solutions work.

541
00:20:26,910 --> 00:20:29,230
We hope this solution meets your needs and expectations.

542
00:20:29,910 --> 00:20:30,780
Positive closing.

543
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:31,980
Polite way to end.

544
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:35,020
Expresses a desire to satisfy their requirements.

545
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:37,030
Aims for a good outcome for them.

546
00:20:37,050 --> 00:20:40,610
And the final one, combining gratitude and anticipation.

547
00:20:41,530 --> 00:20:43,170
Thank you for considering our request.

548
00:20:43,250 --> 00:20:44,840
We await your favorable reply.

549
00:20:44,950 --> 00:20:50,390
nice closer, expresses thanks for their time and politely signals you're hoping for a yes.

550
00:20:51,110 --> 00:20:52,620
Professional and courteous.

551
00:20:52,850 --> 00:20:53,230
Wow.

552
00:20:53,330 --> 00:20:53,650
Okay.

553
00:20:54,030 --> 00:20:57,230
So that's a pretty deep dive into a lot of phrases.

554
00:20:57,910 --> 00:21:06,060
Hopefully that gives everyone a much better sense of not just what to say in emails, but maybe why these specific phrasings are so common and effective.

555
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:06,600
Yeah.

556
00:21:06,620 --> 00:21:07,600
And it's interesting, isn't it?

557
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:10,370
Mastering these isn't just about tweaking individual emails.

558
00:21:10,410 --> 00:21:11,730
It's like bigger than that.

559
00:21:11,750 --> 00:21:12,390
How do you mean?

560
00:21:12,410 --> 00:21:18,770
Well, it's about building those stronger professional relationships, you know, creating trust, communicating more effectively overall.

561
00:21:18,830 --> 00:21:21,660
It's understanding the subtle influence your words have.

562
00:21:21,640 --> 00:21:24,060
Shaping perceptions, getting things done more smoothly.

563
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:37,660
So it really makes you think, which of these nuances, these phrases, could you start using maybe more intentionally right now, today, to actually see a difference in how your emails land and how efficiently you communicate?

564
00:21:37,700 --> 00:21:38,720
That's the challenge, isn't it?

565
00:21:38,760 --> 00:21:39,980
Putting it into practice.

566
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,660
Definitely something to chew on as you tackle that inbox.

567
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:43,820
Until our next deep dive.

568
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:44,940
Looking forward to it.

569
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:55,910
Business English for emails?

570
00:21:55,930 --> 00:21:57,270
Yeah, those essential phrases.

571
00:21:57,450 --> 00:21:58,110
Exactly.

572
00:21:58,150 --> 00:22:06,760
And our mission today really is to dig into these, figure out which ones give the most bang for your buck, you know, to quickly level up email skills.

573
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:10,160
And understand the why behind them, not just the what.

574
00:22:10,370 --> 00:22:10,660
Right.

575
00:22:11,630 --> 00:22:14,410
Which I guess really hammers home how important they are.

576
00:22:14,730 --> 00:22:15,070
It does.

577
00:22:15,150 --> 00:22:21,900
It reinforces those core ideas for clarity, professionalism, just making business emails work better.

578
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:22,940
Okay, so let's dive in.

579
00:22:23,040 --> 00:22:25,030
First up, gratitude and being prompt.

580
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:27,350
The source highlights, thank you for your prompt response.

581
00:22:27,890 --> 00:22:31,150
We all say thanks, but why call out the promptness specifically?

582
00:22:31,210 --> 00:22:32,480
Well, it's more than just manners, right?

583
00:22:32,880 --> 00:22:36,560
Acknowledging speed, it subtly reinforces that good behavior.

584
00:22:36,700 --> 00:22:39,370
Someone's quick, you notice it, they feel appreciated.

585
00:22:39,390 --> 00:22:41,010
Makes them more likely to be quick next time.

586
00:22:41,030 --> 00:22:41,890
Potentially, yeah.

587
00:22:42,250 --> 00:22:46,740
And it just shows you value their time in today's crazy inbox world.

588
00:22:47,720 --> 00:22:48,340
Sets a good rhythm.

589
00:22:48,530 --> 00:22:49,410
That makes sense.

590
00:22:49,430 --> 00:22:50,370
Positive reinforcement.

591
00:22:50,510 --> 00:22:52,410
Okay, what about keeping meetings organized?

592
00:22:53,010 --> 00:22:55,450
I am writing to confirm our meeting schedule.

593
00:22:56,390 --> 00:22:57,040
Very direct.

594
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:58,800
Yeah, and that directness is key.

595
00:22:58,900 --> 00:22:59,740
No ambiguity.

596
00:22:59,900 --> 00:23:02,260
It tells the recipient immediately why you're emailing.

597
00:23:02,380 --> 00:23:07,310
So they can process it fast, check their calendar, flag issues, cuts down confusion later.

598
00:23:07,430 --> 00:23:07,870
Exactly.

599
00:23:07,970 --> 00:23:10,650
Respects their time, gets everyone aligned.

600
00:23:10,730 --> 00:23:16,440
And sort of building on that, we also have let's schedule a follow up call to discuss progress.

601
00:23:16,740 --> 00:23:19,540
And are you available for a brief meeting next week?

602
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:21,220
These feel quite proactive.

603
00:23:21,350 --> 00:23:22,010
They really are.

604
00:23:22,050 --> 00:23:24,970
They show you're thinking ahead, planning the next step.

605
00:23:25,230 --> 00:23:26,430
Let's schedule a follow up.

606
00:23:27,130 --> 00:23:28,250
Keeps the momentum going.

607
00:23:28,290 --> 00:23:29,610
Suggests you're on top of things.

608
00:23:29,890 --> 00:23:30,080
Right.

609
00:23:30,390 --> 00:23:32,660
And asking, are you available for a brief meeting?

610
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:35,120
That little word brief is smart.

611
00:23:35,780 --> 00:23:37,160
It signals you won't waste their time.

612
00:23:37,140 --> 00:23:38,460
Makes them more likely to say yes.

613
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:39,550
Okay, good point.

614
00:23:39,730 --> 00:23:40,570
Now, sharing info.

615
00:23:41,030 --> 00:23:43,110
Please find attached the latest project report.

616
00:23:43,190 --> 00:23:44,470
We must write this daily.

617
00:23:44,730 --> 00:23:45,990
Why is it so standard?

618
00:23:46,190 --> 00:23:47,520
It's just predictable.

619
00:23:47,800 --> 00:23:48,100
Clear.

620
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:52,000
Everyone knows exactly what it means, where to look for the document, what it is.

621
00:23:52,420 --> 00:23:54,640
Reduces the mental effort, I guess.

622
00:23:55,020 --> 00:23:56,720
Less chance of missing the attachment.

623
00:23:56,830 --> 00:23:57,290
Precisely.

624
00:23:57,590 --> 00:23:59,170
Familiarity helps efficiency.

625
00:23:59,650 --> 00:24:01,160
And then there's keeping people updated.

626
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:07,990
I'm reaching out to update you on our project status, or I will update you once more information becomes available.

627
00:24:08,110 --> 00:24:08,890
Transparency.

628
00:24:08,910 --> 00:24:09,120
Right.

629
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:10,280
Super important.

630
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:14,510
The first one shows you're proactively informing stakeholders, building trust.

631
00:24:14,660 --> 00:24:16,440
Even if there's no massive news.

632
00:24:16,540 --> 00:24:17,660
Especially then sometimes.

633
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:20,100
And the second one, I'll update you when I know more.

634
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:22,570
That manages expectations.

635
00:24:22,750 --> 00:24:26,310
It stops people chasing you unnecessarily because they know you haven't forgotten.

636
00:24:26,510 --> 00:24:26,710
Right.

637
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:28,720
Prevents those, any updates yet.

638
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:29,780
Emails.

639
00:24:30,600 --> 00:24:30,860
Okay.

640
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:32,660
Next up, needing clarification.

641
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:35,970
Could you please clarify the deadline for this deliverable?

642
00:24:36,410 --> 00:24:37,810
How do you ask these things well?

643
00:24:37,790 --> 00:24:38,930
It's that balance, isn't it?

644
00:24:39,450 --> 00:24:40,320
Polite but direct.

645
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:40,920
Could you please?

646
00:24:41,280 --> 00:24:41,820
Softens it.

647
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:42,840
Makes it respectful.

648
00:24:43,080 --> 00:24:45,880
But it still clearly states what you need the deadline.

649
00:24:46,050 --> 00:24:46,530
Exactly.

650
00:24:46,990 --> 00:24:50,650
Much better than being demanding or like really vague about it.

651
00:24:51,070 --> 00:24:51,960
You get a better response.

652
00:24:52,460 --> 00:24:54,540
And what about when you genuinely need advice?

653
00:24:55,260 --> 00:24:57,240
Please advise on the best course of action.

654
00:24:57,790 --> 00:24:59,430
When's the best time to pull that one out?

655
00:24:59,680 --> 00:25:05,070
Well, it's good when you truly value their expertise or maybe they see the bigger picture.

656
00:25:05,450 --> 00:25:06,770
It acknowledges their experience.

657
00:25:06,790 --> 00:25:08,510
But you shouldn't use it as a crutch, right?

658
00:25:08,670 --> 00:25:10,760
You need to have done some thinking yourself.

659
00:25:10,780 --> 00:25:11,460
Oh, absolutely.

660
00:25:11,520 --> 00:25:13,500
You risk looking unprepared otherwise.

661
00:25:14,060 --> 00:25:18,610
Maybe give some context like, here's what I'm thinking, but please advise that works well.

662
00:25:18,910 --> 00:25:19,430
Good tip.

663
00:25:19,990 --> 00:25:20,270
Okay.

664
00:25:20,610 --> 00:25:21,330
Shifting gears.

665
00:25:21,970 --> 00:25:23,370
Relationship building.

666
00:25:23,940 --> 00:25:26,970
We appreciate your continued support and partnership.

667
00:25:27,510 --> 00:25:29,790
Why is explicitly saying this so important?

668
00:25:30,250 --> 00:25:32,640
Business relies on relationships long-term.

669
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:36,360
Saying things like this reinforces that you value that connection.

670
00:25:36,700 --> 00:25:38,020
It builds goodwill, loyalty.

671
00:25:38,120 --> 00:25:40,110
Makes people feel appreciated, not taken for granted.

672
00:25:40,290 --> 00:25:40,510
Yeah.

673
00:25:40,870 --> 00:25:43,990
Simple phrase, but it shows you recognize their part in your success.

674
00:25:44,310 --> 00:25:46,130
And related to that, getting feedback.

675
00:25:46,490 --> 00:25:48,800
Your feedback on this matter would be greatly valued.

676
00:25:49,160 --> 00:25:51,100
That sounds quite formal, but effective.

677
00:25:51,210 --> 00:25:53,220
The greatly valued part is key.

678
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:57,000
It tells them their opinion really matters, that you'll actually listen.

679
00:25:57,800 --> 00:26:00,790
Makes them more willing to spend the time giving thoughtful input.

680
00:26:00,810 --> 00:26:02,950
Encourages better quality feedback, maybe.

681
00:26:03,010 --> 00:26:03,810
I think so, yeah.

682
00:26:03,830 --> 00:26:09,800
We also saw the more direct, please provide your feedback on the attached proposal for when it's more specific.

683
00:26:09,880 --> 00:26:10,320
Exactly.

684
00:26:10,360 --> 00:26:12,960
It's a clear call to action on a specific item.

685
00:26:13,480 --> 00:26:16,670
Still polite with please, but very direct about what's needed.

686
00:26:17,290 --> 00:26:17,850
Efficient.

687
00:26:17,830 --> 00:26:18,810
Okay.

688
00:26:18,830 --> 00:26:20,700
Now, the not so fun stuff.

689
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:22,200
Bad news.

690
00:26:23,010 --> 00:26:25,860
We regret to inform you of a delay in our timeline.

691
00:26:26,460 --> 00:26:28,220
How do you handle these professionally?

692
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:36,050
Clarity and a bit of formality help, we regret, acknowledges the negative impact directly but professionally.

693
00:26:36,190 --> 00:26:37,510
Without getting overly emotional.

694
00:26:37,690 --> 00:26:37,930
Right.

695
00:26:38,290 --> 00:26:43,800
And crucially, you must follow it up immediately with the why, what you're doing about it, and any new timeline.

696
00:26:43,930 --> 00:26:45,420
Just dropping the bad news isn't enough.

697
00:26:45,440 --> 00:26:47,000
Definitely need the context and the plan.

698
00:26:47,180 --> 00:26:48,680
Okay, confirming things got there.

699
00:26:49,020 --> 00:26:50,760
Please confirm receipt of this email.

700
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:52,850
When is this absolutely necessary?

701
00:26:52,940 --> 00:27:01,520
It's critical when you're sending something really important, sensitive info, stuff needing urgent action, key attachments, documents tied to deadlines.

702
00:27:01,740 --> 00:27:04,420
Gives you that peace of mind it didn't vanish into the ether.

703
00:27:04,500 --> 00:27:05,040
Exactly.

704
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:06,220
Just confirms it landed.

705
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:08,290
And then the action-focused ones.

706
00:27:08,930 --> 00:27:16,640
Please confirm if the attached terms are acceptable to you, or please review and sign the attached contract at your earliest convenience.

707
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:18,880
These are about pushing things forward.

708
00:27:18,950 --> 00:27:21,330
Yeah, very clearly asking for the next step.

709
00:27:21,930 --> 00:27:23,860
Confirming terms needs a clear yes-no.

710
00:27:24,460 --> 00:27:26,770
Reviewing and signing a contract, well, that's obvious.

711
00:27:27,330 --> 00:27:31,460
And the at your earliest convenience adds that polite nudge for speed.

712
00:27:31,780 --> 00:27:32,080
Right.

713
00:27:32,340 --> 00:27:33,900
Making communication easy.

714
00:27:34,150 --> 00:27:37,170
I am available to discuss this at your earliest convenience.

715
00:27:37,190 --> 00:27:39,100
Offering flexibility seems smart.

716
00:27:39,380 --> 00:27:39,640
It is.

717
00:27:40,080 --> 00:27:45,270
It shows you're willing to work around their schedule, makes the conversation feel less demanding, more collaborative.

718
00:27:45,380 --> 00:27:47,530
Increases the chance they'll actually schedule that discussion.

719
00:27:47,610 --> 00:27:48,710
Probably, yeah.

720
00:27:48,730 --> 00:27:49,530
Okay, good news time.

721
00:27:50,250 --> 00:27:52,830
We are pleased to announce the launch of our new product.

722
00:27:53,310 --> 00:27:54,120
Nice and positive.

723
00:27:54,620 --> 00:27:56,400
How do you share these wins professionally?

724
00:27:56,860 --> 00:27:57,760
Just like that, really.

725
00:27:58,160 --> 00:28:00,580
Clear, concise, positive tone.

726
00:28:00,560 --> 00:28:05,930
We are pleased to announce, get straight to the good news, great for informing everyone and building a bit of buzz.

727
00:28:05,990 --> 00:28:06,210
Right.

728
00:28:06,710 --> 00:28:07,590
Now, follow-ups.

729
00:28:08,030 --> 00:28:08,830
We all need to do them.

730
00:28:09,450 --> 00:28:11,360
I would like to follow up on our previous discussion.

731
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:12,200
Simple.

732
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:13,340
Direct.

733
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:15,460
What makes a good follow-up?

734
00:28:15,480 --> 00:28:16,100
Keep it brief.

735
00:28:16,810 --> 00:28:18,230
Remind them what you discussed before.

736
00:28:18,490 --> 00:28:20,130
Clearly state why you're following up now.

737
00:28:20,650 --> 00:28:22,850
This phrase does that job perfectly.

738
00:28:23,510 --> 00:28:24,380
Polite prompt.

739
00:28:24,610 --> 00:28:31,120
And then there are reminders, sometimes slightly awkward ones, like this is a gentle reminder regarding our pending invoice.

740
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:32,620
How do you nail that tone?

741
00:28:32,700 --> 00:28:35,140
That word gentle is doing a lot of work there, isn't it?

742
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:36,990
Yeah, it softens it considerably.

743
00:28:37,010 --> 00:28:37,550
Exactly.

744
00:28:37,970 --> 00:28:39,550
Avoid sounding accusatory.

745
00:28:39,570 --> 00:28:43,320
It's professional, prompts action, but hopefully doesn't damage the relationship.

746
00:28:43,780 --> 00:28:45,240
Polite and factual is the way.

747
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:45,900
Good advice.

748
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:47,960
Recognizing people's work.

749
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:50,230
Your assistance in this project has been invaluable.

750
00:28:50,690 --> 00:28:51,470
That feels strong.

751
00:28:51,770 --> 00:28:54,310
What's the payoff for being so direct with praise?

752
00:28:54,530 --> 00:28:56,330
Huge impact, potentially.

753
00:28:56,350 --> 00:29:05,500
It shows you genuinely see and appreciate their effort, their contribution, boosts morale, strengthens the team, makes people feel properly recognized.

754
00:29:05,640 --> 00:29:07,490
Yeah, feeling valued is massive.

755
00:29:07,990 --> 00:29:09,030
Okay, urgency.

756
00:29:09,470 --> 00:29:10,830
Sometimes you just need things that we have.

757
00:29:11,270 --> 00:29:13,680
I would appreciate your urgent attention to this matter.

758
00:29:13,860 --> 00:29:16,100
Or could you send me the details by the end of the day?

759
00:29:16,340 --> 00:29:19,400
Or even please prioritize this task as it is critical.

760
00:29:19,380 --> 00:29:21,030
How do you rush people politely?

761
00:29:21,210 --> 00:29:21,430
Uh-huh.

762
00:29:21,630 --> 00:29:22,580
Yeah, the polite rush.

763
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:28,210
It's about being clear on the need for speed and ideally explaining why it's urgent.

764
00:29:28,350 --> 00:29:31,150
So critical to our success gives that context.

765
00:29:31,270 --> 00:29:31,850
Exactly.

766
00:29:32,150 --> 00:29:36,780
Phrases like urgent attention or please prioritize state the need directly.

767
00:29:37,320 --> 00:29:45,590
Specific requests like by end of day or expedite the documentation, set clear expectations, but keep the please and appreciate in there.

768
00:29:45,950 --> 00:29:47,450
Tone matters, even when urgent.

769
00:29:47,530 --> 00:29:47,730
Right.

770
00:29:47,910 --> 00:29:48,810
Don't want to sound demanding.

771
00:29:48,870 --> 00:29:49,110
Okay.

772
00:29:49,130 --> 00:29:49,850
Communicating goals.

773
00:29:50,590 --> 00:29:55,720
We are committed to delivering top quality results or we need to revise our strategy to better meet our goals.

774
00:29:56,100 --> 00:29:57,340
Why put these in emails?

775
00:29:57,470 --> 00:30:07,660
It sets expectations, communicate company values or direction, committed to top quality, reinforces standards, revise our strategy, shows adaptability and focus on goals.

776
00:30:08,090 --> 00:30:09,270
It helps align everyone.

777
00:30:09,370 --> 00:30:09,810
Makes sense.

778
00:30:10,270 --> 00:30:11,410
Endings are important too.

779
00:30:11,770 --> 00:30:13,710
Thank you for your understanding and cooperation.

780
00:30:14,090 --> 00:30:16,080
Common closing, why does it work so well?

781
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:21,920
It's just polite, professional, especially good after you've asked for something or maybe explained a small issue.

782
00:30:23,300 --> 00:30:27,890
It acknowledges their willingness to work with you, leaves a good final impression.

783
00:30:28,150 --> 00:30:29,150
Fosters that goodwill.

784
00:30:29,370 --> 00:30:29,590
Okay.

785
00:30:30,230 --> 00:30:31,950
What about needing specific skills?

786
00:30:32,630 --> 00:30:35,180
It would be beneficial to have your expertise on this matter.

787
00:30:35,560 --> 00:30:36,880
Respectful way to ask.

788
00:30:36,990 --> 00:30:37,430
Very.

789
00:30:37,820 --> 00:30:40,400
It focuses on the benefit their expertise brings.

790
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:44,430
It values their knowledge, invites them in without demanding, makes them feel valued.

791
00:30:44,530 --> 00:30:46,390
And for scheduling, offering choices.

792
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:49,160
Let us know if the proposed dates are suitable for you.

793
00:30:49,400 --> 00:30:50,400
Feels more collaborative.

794
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:50,800
It is.

795
00:30:50,880 --> 00:30:51,670
It gives them agency.

796
00:30:51,790 --> 00:30:55,250
Shows you're considering their schedule, willing to find a time that works for both.

797
00:30:55,890 --> 00:30:56,760
Less dictatorial.

798
00:30:56,790 --> 00:30:57,150
Right.

799
00:30:57,490 --> 00:30:58,370
OK, apologies.

800
00:30:58,630 --> 00:30:59,670
We all mess up sometimes.

801
00:31:00,070 --> 00:31:03,080
I apologize for any inconvenience this may have caused.

802
00:31:03,100 --> 00:31:05,430
Why is taking responsibility like this so vital?

803
00:31:05,730 --> 00:31:06,970
Trust, basically.

804
